@node Porting notes
@chapter Porting notes

libdtfl is a port, not a reimplementation.  This chapter records how it
relates to the Rust originals, for a reader who knows them.

@section The pin is the API

The port targets released code, not moving heads:

@table @asis
@item timely dataflow
@code{timely-v0.31.0}.
@item differential dataflow
@code{differential-dataflow-v0.25.1}, which builds against timely 0.31.
@item the diagnostics tools
the @command{tdiag} computations, retargeted to read the timely 0.31 log
format.
@end table

@strong{The pin is the API.}  Everything public at @code{timely-v0.31.0}
is ported, @emph{including the operators upstream deleted after the
pin}: @code{Notificator}, @code{FrontierNotificator}, @code{unary_notify},
@code{binary_notify}, @code{delay}, @code{delay_total},
@code{delay_batch}, @code{branch}, @code{branch_when}, @code{aggregate},
@code{state_machine}, @code{accumulate}, @code{count}, @code{reclock},
@code{iterator_source}, the @code{Result} stream helpers, and the
@code{inspect} variants.  Code at the pin depends on them (differential's
@code{Collection::delay} is built on @code{delay_batch}, which is built
on @code{unary_notify}), and porting the upstream tests requires them.
Where this manual shows one of these, it notes that it was removed
upstream after timely 0.31.

@section Mapping Rust idioms to C

@table @asis
@item Typed records become byte strings.
Rust gets typed records from monomorphisation.  libdtfl represents a
record as a byte string with a @code{dtfl_type} descriptor
(@pxref{Records}); serialization for exchange is a @code{memcpy}, which
removes Rust's @code{ExchangeData} bound and the cost of bincode.
@item Closures become a function pointer and a context.
Every operator that takes a closure in Rust takes a function pointer and
a @code{dtfl_ctx} (a pointer plus an optional destructor) in C.  A
context passed with a destructor is owned by the operator and freed when
the operator is dropped.
@item Ownership becomes explicit free functions.
Each handle has exactly one function that frees or drops it:
@code{dtfl_cap_drop}, @code{dtfl_collection_free},
@code{dtfl_input_session_close}, @code{dtfl_arranged_free}, and so on.
The @code{!Send} types of Rust become the worker-ownership rule of
@ref{Threads}.
@item The diff algebra is checked at build time.
Rust enforces the trait bounds (Semigroup, Monoid, Abelian) at compile
time.  libdtfl checks them when the dataflow is built and reports
@code{DTFL_EALGEBRA}, naming the operator, when a diff class lacks an
operation an operator needs.
@end table

@section Divergences from the pin

The port matches the pin.  Where it does not, there are exactly two
deliberate differences:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Byte aliasing.}  The byte-sharing code takes the aliasing and
regeneration hardening from timely #821, which landed after the pin.
#821 fixes a soundness hole in the Rust @code{Bytes} type; porting the
pre-fix code on purpose would be porting a bug.
@item
@strong{Diagnostics bind address.}  The diagnostics WebSocket server
binds @code{127.0.0.1} by default, where upstream binds @code{0.0.0.0}
and so publishes operator names and timings to the network with no
authentication.  The address is a parameter, so the upstream behavior is
one argument away (@pxref{Diagnostics}).
@end enumerate
